Dear Luciano:

Thank you for this syntax. It is intriguing.

But when I run it, I don't get a least squares line but rather a piecewise linear continuous interpolation. Did you get single line? See attachment for the plot I got. This is the result of the following code:

set.seed(7)

DUT<- data.frame( stuff=round(rnorm(7), 2))

ggplot(data = DUT, aes(sample = stuff)) + stat_qq() + geom_smooth(method = "lm", aes(x = ..theoretical.., y = ..sample..), stat = "qq")

I don't understand why geom_smooth called in that way would make a piecewise linear interpolation. If we take stat_qq out of the picture it behaves as we might expect:

set.seed(7)

junk<-data.frame(pred=rnorm(7))

junk$resp<- junk$pred * 1.3 + rnorm(7)/5 + 1

ggplot(data=junk, mapping=aes(x=pred, y=resp)) + geom_point() + geom_smooth(method="lm", aes(x=pred, y=resp), se=FALSE)

Jake

